WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.360
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:05.520
Let's build a field from its sources rather than guess a formula from the

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:05.740
shape.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.160
For point charges, we add electric field vectors.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:14.860
A continuous distribution uses the same idea with tiny charge elements, called

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:16.240
d q.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.848
Each contribution points along the source to observation direction for

00:00:19.848 --> 00:00:20.260
positive

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:20.640
charge.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.180
Integrate components, not just positive magnitudes.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.360
For a line, d q is line density times a small length.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:32.280
Surface and volume densities need area and volume elements instead.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:34.600
Those units are not interchangeable.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.960
Now picture a thin uniformly charged ring, radius a, total charge Q.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.980
Our point lies a distance x along its central perpendicular axis.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.540
All ring elements have the same distance to that point, the square root of a

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:49.640
squared plus x squared.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:51.360
Pair opposite elements.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.460
Their sideways field components cancel, while axial components add.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.880
The axial projection supplies a factor x divided by that same distance.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:05.400
Integrating the charge gives axial field k Q x divided by a squared plus x

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.940
squared to the power three halves.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.760
The sign of x matters: for positive Q, field points away from the centre on

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:12.340
either side.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.920
Potential is easier because it is a scalar.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:20.358
With zero at infinity, it is k Q divided by the square root of a squared plus

00:01:20.358 --> 00:01:20.420
x

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:20.660
squared.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:26.340
Try a numerical example: one nanocoulomb, radius zero point three metres, and

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.140
axial x zero point four metres.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.940
Use k equal to nine times ten to the ninth in SI units.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:33.880
Distance is half a metre.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:38.740
Potential is eighteen volts, and field is twenty eight point eight newtons per

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.080
coulomb along positive x.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:42.440
Now think about the centre.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:46.728
Field is zero by cancellation, but potential is positive, thirty volts for

00:01:46.728 --> 00:01:46.960
this

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:47.140
ring.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.800
Zero field does not mean zero potential.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:54.280
Differentiating potential with respect to x and changing the sign reproduces

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.300
the axial field, a useful check.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.760
Far away, the ring behaves approximately like a point charge.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:01.940
Close to it, keep the geometry.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:06.520
Symmetry simplifies an integral; it does not replace the need to identify the

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:07.100
correct components.

00:02:07.512 --> 00:02:13.272
Next, put a uniform thin insulating rod on the x axis from minus a to plus a.

00:02:13.592 --> 00:02:16.272
Observe a point a positive distance y above its midpoint.

00:02:17.152 --> 00:02:19.672
Elements on opposite sides cancel horizontal components.

00:02:20.292 --> 00:02:22.632
Their upward components add for positive charge.

00:02:23.212 --> 00:02:27.760
A source element at coordinate s contributes k times line density times y

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:28.072
times

00:02:28.072 --> 00:02:32.112
d s, divided by s squared plus y squared to the power three halves.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:34.772
Integrate from minus a to plus a.

00:02:34.772 --> 00:02:39.892
The result is two k times line density times a, divided by y times the square

00:02:39.892 --> 00:02:41.512
root of a squared plus y squared.

00:02:42.392 --> 00:02:46.712
Take a zero point three metres, y zero point four metres and line density two

00:02:46.712 --> 00:02:47.692
nanocoulombs per metre.

00:02:48.172 --> 00:02:50.272
We get fifty four newtons per coulomb upward.

00:02:51.052 --> 00:02:54.852
If length becomes very large while line density stays fixed, the end position

00:02:54.852 --> 00:02:57.672
factor approaches one, giving the infinite line field.

00:02:58.652 --> 00:03:01.672
Keeping total charge fixed instead changes line density and is a different

00:03:01.672 --> 00:03:01.912
limit.

00:03:02.472 --> 00:03:06.392
A finite rod does not have the translational symmetry needed for a simple

00:03:06.392 --> 00:03:07.992
cylindrical Gauss law field shortcut.

00:03:08.972 --> 00:03:09.572
Now a dipole.

00:03:10.392 --> 00:03:14.752
Put minus one nanocoulomb at x minus zero point one metres and plus one

00:03:14.752 --> 00:03:17.192
nanocoulomb at x plus zero point one metres.

00:03:17.852 --> 00:03:20.712
Our observation point is x plus zero point three metres.

00:03:21.592 --> 00:03:24.012
Distances are zero point two and zero point four metres.

00:03:24.312 --> 00:03:27.872
The positive charge contributes two hundred twenty five newtons per coulomb

00:03:27.872 --> 00:03:31.932
rightward; the negative charge contributes fifty six point two five leftward.

00:03:31.932 --> 00:03:36.472
Subtract: exact net field is one hundred sixty eight point seven five newtons

00:03:36.472 --> 00:03:37.392
per coulomb rightward.

00:03:38.192 --> 00:03:42.132
The dipole moment is charge magnitude times separation, two times ten to the

00:03:42.132 --> 00:03:43.292
minus ten coulomb metres.

00:03:43.772 --> 00:03:48.575
The familiar far axis formula, two k p divided by r cubed, gives about one

00:03:48.575 --> 00:03:50.392
hundred thirty three point three three.

00:03:50.392 --> 00:03:53.272
That is about twenty one percent below the exact result here.

00:03:53.272 --> 00:03:57.892
The exact axial expression is two k p r divided by the square of r squared

00:03:57.892 --> 00:04:00.052
minus a squared, for r greater than a.

00:04:00.672 --> 00:04:04.192
It becomes the far field formula only when r is much larger than a.

00:04:04.972 --> 00:04:07.728
A convenient formula needs a valid approximation, not just familiar symbols.

00:04:07.728 --> 00:04:10.588
Let's finish on the dipole's perpendicular bisector.

00:04:11.428 --> 00:04:15.528
A point there is equally distant from positive and negative charges, so their

00:04:15.528 --> 00:04:18.968
scalar potentials cancel exactly, choosing zero at infinity.

00:04:19.348 --> 00:04:21.028
But the fields do not cancel.

00:04:22.248 --> 00:04:26.188
Components along the bisector cancel; components along the dipole axis point

00:04:26.188 --> 00:04:28.268
from positive toward negative and add.

00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:30.968
The net field is opposite the dipole moment.

00:04:30.968 --> 00:04:36.568
Its exact magnitude is k p divided by r squared plus a squared to the power

00:04:36.568 --> 00:04:37.148
three halves.

00:04:38.088 --> 00:04:41.008
Far away this approaches k p divided by r cubed.

00:04:41.648 --> 00:04:44.488
Once again, zero potential does not imply zero field.

00:04:45.168 --> 00:04:47.928
Now place a fixed dipole in a uniform external field.

00:04:48.868 --> 00:04:52.508
Equal opposite forces give zero net force, but generally form a torque.

00:04:53.388 --> 00:04:55.648
Torque magnitude is p E sine theta.

00:04:56.388 --> 00:05:01.368
Orientation energy is minus p E cosine theta, choosing zero when the dipole is

00:05:01.368 --> 00:05:01.908
perpendicular.

00:05:02.928 --> 00:05:06.508
Parallel alignment has minimum energy and is stable; opposite alignment has

00:05:06.508 --> 00:05:08.208
maximum energy and is unstable.

00:05:08.988 --> 00:05:09.848
Let's work a rotation.

00:05:10.588 --> 00:05:14.193
Charge magnitude is two nanocoulombs and separation zero point zero two

00:05:14.193 --> 00:05:14.548
metres,

00:05:15.128 --> 00:05:18.548
so dipole moment is four times ten to the minus eleven coulomb metres.

00:05:19.188 --> 00:05:22.188
External field is two times ten to the fifth newtons per coulomb.

00:05:23.068 --> 00:05:25.008
Rotate slowly from parallel to perpendicular.

00:05:25.608 --> 00:05:27.068
What sign should external work have?

00:05:27.068 --> 00:05:28.288
Think first.

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:32.828
You move away from the minimum energy orientation, so stored energy increases.

00:05:33.488 --> 00:05:36.008
The increase is p E, eight microjoules.

00:05:36.788 --> 00:05:40.468
External work is plus eight microjoules, while field work is minus eight.

00:05:41.008 --> 00:05:44.488
We assumed negligible kinetic energy change and no other transfers.

00:05:45.368 --> 00:05:48.528
Zero torque at the starting alignment does not mean zero work over the entire

00:05:48.528 --> 00:05:48.928
rotation.

00:05:49.428 --> 00:05:51.688
And a zero net force does not mean zero torque.

00:05:52.308 --> 00:05:56.568
For JEE, state the source geometry, whether a far field approximation applies,

00:05:56.568 --> 00:05:59.348
the potential reference and whose work is requested.

00:06:00.028 --> 00:06:03.108
Then the signs and equations tell one consistent physical story.
